          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#summary"} Summary
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview_of_the_paper"} Overview of the paper
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_fortunes_and_misfortunes_of_euclids_units"} The fortunes and misfortunes of Euclid’s units
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#links"} Links
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#summary Summary
          %p This page collects material related to the article
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
              %p
                %em> Cohesive Toposes and Cantor’s “lauter Einsen”
                ,
              %p Philosophia Mathematica (3) Vol. 2 (1994) pp. 5-15.
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/2.1.5"} doi:10.1093/philmat/2.1.5
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereCohesiveToposes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+cylinders"} adjoint cylinders
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+toposes"} cohesive toposes
          %h2#overview_of_the_paper Overview of the paper
          %p
            The article claims that
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Cantor1895"} Cantor 1895
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat X
            of a cohesive type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Beyond this, the article is in part motivated from attempts to formalize thoughts appearing in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
          %p
            Concretely it is about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            that in a set all elements are “distinct, but indistinguishable”. Lawvere argues that this and Cantor’s original notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            is captured by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %p
            This seems to match well with
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %em
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#PureQuantity"} Pure quantity
            both informally as well as formally (as discussed there).
          %p
            Notice that where the English translations of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            say “the One”, the original has “Das Eins”, which might just as well be translated with “The Unit”. In view of this and looking through Hegel’s piece on discreteness and repulsion, it seems clear that Hegel’s “Einsen” is precisely Cantor’s “Einsen” as recalled by Lawvere. Namely: copies of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
          %p Notice that the German “lauter” is pretty much the English “lots of”. When you say “A lot o'” as in the slang “A lot o' stuff” or else say “lauter” with a Bavarian accent, it comes out pretty much alike.
          %p (? The German expression ‘lauter’ predominantly denotes universal quantification, so that the Cantor quote translates ‘all (of) units’. There might in fact be an etymological connection to English ‘lot’ preserved in the meaning ‘(specific) quantity’ - German ‘Posten’ like in e.g. ‘yesterday arrived two lots of cd-players’)
          %p
            On page 7, Lawvere makes a distinction between
            %em objective
            and
            %em subjective
            cohesion. The subjective version involve the “coming to know” aspects of points in a space. “A good example of a type of set possessing subjective cohesion is that of recursive set, which is ‘traced’ by various threads generated by particular recursive functions.”
          %p In view of the relationship between cohesion and modality, Lawvere’s distinction should correspond to that between metaphysical and epistemic modalities (“As far as I know, it may be the case that…”).
          %p
            On page 11, Lawvere discusses categories intermediate between the original cohesive category and the base. This relates to the discussion, pp. 10-11 of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"} Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
          %h2#the_fortunes_and_misfortunes_of_euclids_units The fortunes and misfortunes of Euclid’s units
          %p
            It is noteworthy that Cantor’s conception of cardinal number is a mildly disguised form of the definition of number as
            %em ‘a multitude made up of units’
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"} Euclid
            %em finite
            cardinals and in Cantor’s time was e.g. used by the highly influential Karl Weierstrass in his lectures
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            and defended by Weierstrass’ student Edmund Husserl in his ‘Philosophie der Arithmetik’ (1891).
          %p
            Cantor comments on Euclid in ‘
            %em> Mitteilungen zur Lehre vom Transfiniten
            ’ (1887-88,
            %a{:href => "#Cantor32"} Cantor (1932)
            pp.378-438)). His comment (p.380f.) makes it clear that he views cardinals actually as the cohesive ‘united’ objects whereas he
            %em stresses the discreteness
            of the concrete elements in the ‘Mengen’. He criticizes Euclid for not emphasizing that numbers are actually more unified objects than the material sets they are abstracted from. Another key passage (with unmistakingly Aristotelian overtones) in this direction reads:
          %blockquote
            %p
              ‘
              %em>
                Die Elemente der uns gegenüberstehenden Menge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                sind getrennt vorzustellen; in dem intellektualem Gebilde
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi M
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{M}
              ’ (p.380)
          %p In a letter to Peano he writes:
          %blockquote
            %p
              ‘
              %em I conceive of numbers as ‘forms’ or ‘species’ (general concepts) of sets. In essentials this is the conception of the ancient geometry of Plato, Aristotle, Euclid etc.’
              (Cantor,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Briefe
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Hill00"} Hill (2000)
          %p
            What underlies the contrast between Mengen and Kardinalen, is a platonist conception of mathematics. By abstracting from the contingencies of the material sets one reaches a realm of ideas of higher reality as they achieve a better unification of the many into unity; in particular, the introduction of the units acts as a uniformisation and homogenisation of the diversity-matter of the concrete sets which are then able to participate in the idea of the cardinal number. The pertinent contrast in Cantor is between concrete-particular vs. abstract-general. Moreover, it seems, that though cardinals are indeed (related to) ‘abstract sets’, a general concept of ‘abstract set’ is absent here, as on Cantor’s view there can only be one abstract set of a given cardinality i.e. according to Cantor there is something like THE number 2. It seems also somewhat paradoxically to come up with rudimentary structural set theory in order
            %em to do transfinite arithmetic
            \!
          %p
            The views advanced in R. Dedekind’s ‘
            %em> Was sind und was sollen die Zahlen?
            ’ (1887) appear to be a more decisive step towards the modern structural approach to set theory and mathematics. Though he similarly defines the natural numbers in §6.73 by abstraction as a certain ‘simple infinite set’, he clearly has a concept of such a system up to isomorphism: he has also a more general view of ‘abstract set’ (see also the quote in the footnote at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %p
            The abstractionist view was severely criticized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottlob+Frege"} Gottlob Frege
            in
            %em ‘Die Grundlagen der Arithmetik’
            (1884) and his reviews of Cantor and Husserl (1892,1894). Frege rejected the process of
            %em ‘abstraction’
            involved in such accounts and held views on identity that made the ‘distinct yet indistinguishable units’ an inconsistent concept and spoke therefore sarcastically of
            %em ‘unglückliche Einsen’
            \- ‘unfortunate units’: the adjective has stuck in this context as we find it repeated in Zermelo’s and Tait’s discussion of Cantor’s conception.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beitr%C3%A4ge+zur+Begr%C3%BCndung+der+transfiniten+Mengenlehre"} Beiträge zur Begründung der transfiniten Mengenlehre
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material-structural+adjunction"} material-structural adjunction
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                ncafé discussion: Rethinking Set Theory ? (
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2012/12/rethinking_set_theory.html"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                Francois Dorais : Back to Cantor ? (
                %a{:href => "http://logic.dorais.org/archives/1135"}> link
                )
          %h2#references References
          %p Lawvere takes this interpretation of Cantor up again in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Foundations and Applications: Axiomatization and Education
              %strong 9
              no.2 (2003) pp.213-224. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.math.ucla.edu/~asl/bsl/0902/0902-006.ps"}> ps-preprint
              )
          %p
            He gives prominence to the essentiality of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation topos
            and the “Cantor comonad” that comes with it in the more recent
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Birkhoff’s theorem from a geometric
              %p
                perspective: A simple example_ , Categories and General Algebraic Structures with Applications
                %strong 4
                no.1 (2015) pp.1–7. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cgasa.ir/pdf_12425_5ddf468d82aad53ce2e8ee88dd3bf84d.html"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            Judging from the preface to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} (Lawvere-Rosebrugh 2003)
            Myhill made his observation presumably in Lawvere’s 1985 course. C. McLarty interprets Cantor’s cardinals as abstract sets in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                ,
                %em Defining Sets as Sets of Points of Spaces
                , JPL
                %strong 17
                (1988) pp.75-90.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elementary+Categories%2C+Elementary+Toposes"} Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes
                , Oxford UP 1992 (
                %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/elementary-categories-elementary-toposes-9780198514732?cc=ae&lang=en&"}> ISBN:9780198514732
                )
          %p The Cantor text Lawvere refers to is
          %ul
            %li#Cantor1895
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"}> Georg Cantor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beitr%C3%A4ge+zur+Begr%C3%BCndung+der+transfiniten+Mengenlehre"} Beiträge zur Begründung der transfiniten Mengenlehre
              %strong 46
              (1895) pp.481-512. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/img/?PPN=GDZPPN00225557X"}> German original
              ;
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/contributionstof00cant"} online English translation
          %p The text is reprinted on pp.282-351 of Cantor’s collected works and the revelant comment by Zermelo is in the second annotation on p.351:
          %ul
            %li#Cantor32
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
              (ed.),
              %em Georg Cantor - Gesammelte Abhandlungen Mathematischen und Philosophischen Inhalts
              , Springer Berlin 1932. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/toc/?PPN=PPN237853094"}> gdz
              )
          %p An English version of Cantor’s 1885 review of Frege appears as
          %ul
            %li
              P. A. Ebert, M. Rossberg,
              %em Cantor on Frege’s Foundations of Arthmetic
              , Hist. Phil. Logic
              %strong 30
              no.4 (2010) pp.341-348. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.philipebert.info/resources/Cantorreview_Final.pdf"}> draft
              )
          %p For an extensive discussion and relevant quotes of Cantor’s platonism and abstractionism see
          %ul
            %li#Hill00
              C. O. Hill,
              %em Abstraction and Idealization in Edmund Husserl and Georg Cantor prior to 1895
              , in Hill, Haddock (eds.),
              %em Husserl or Frege, Meaning, Objectivity and Mathematics
              , Open Court Chicago 2000.
          %p For a recent controversial discussion of Cantorian abstraction, the second article using MLTT:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                K. Fine,
                %em Cantorian abstraction: A reconstruction and defense
                , J. Phil.
                %strong 95
                no.12 (1998) pp.599-634.
            %li
              %p
                W. Hinzen,
                %em Constructive versus Ontological Construals of Cantorian Ordinals
                , Hist. Phil. Logic
                %strong 24
                no.1 (2003) pp.45-63.
          %p
            The key concepts of ‘one’ and ‘many’ taken from Plato’s ‘
            %em> Philebus
            ’ dialogue reappear in Hegel’s “set theory”:
          %ul
            %li
              Alan L. T. Paterson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Frege and Hegel on concepts and number
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://sites.google.com/site/apat1erson/nnfregh.pdf?attredirects=0"} pdf
          %p For more on the Frege-Cantor debate on the concept of cardinal number, which touches upon the status of structural mathematics more generally, see
          %ul
            %li#Tait96
              William W. Tait,
              %em Frege versus Cantor and Dedekind: On the Concept of Number
              , pp.213-248 in Tait (ed.),
              %em Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein: Essays in Early Analytic Philosophy
              , Lasalle Open Court Press 1996. (
              %a{:href => "http://home.uchicago.edu/%7Ewwtx/frege.cantor.dedekind.pdf"}> preprint
              )
          %p For a proposal that contrasts Cantorian set theory with Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory (though leaving out the question of abstract sets) see
          %ul
            %li
              J. Lake,
              %em The Approaches to Set Theory
              , Notre Dame J. Formal Logic
              %strong 20
              no.2 (1979) pp.415-437. (
              %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf_1/euclid.ndjfl/1093882550"}> pdf
              )
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/reference"} reference
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  It seems somewhat ironical that Weierstrass whose quest for arithmetical foundations of analysis was one of the main driving forces behind 19th mathematics’ move towards set-theoretic foundations actually held views on his fundamental concept ‘number’ that are closely related to a structural view!
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
